# Chapter One — One Person There

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 1 — Calder Investigations, late afternoon

= Present: Henry Calder, Ruth Ellery.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth needs Henry to accept a personal invitation without letting him convert it into professional work.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - LATE AFTERNOON

At five thirty-seven, Henry Calder turns the card in the frosted-glass door from OPEN to CLOSED. It has said CLOSED for most of the afternoon. Turning it is less an announcement than a ritual.

Ruth Ellery knocks directly beneath his painted name.

HENRY
It's open.

RUTH
It says closed.

HENRY
Then why did you knock?

RUTH
I remembered you were a liar.

Henry opens the door. Ruth looks first at him, then at the room: two client chairs, one filing cabinet, a telephone, a fan moving warm air from one side of the office to the other.

RUTH
Your name has gotten smaller.

HENRY
The door was replaced.

RUTH
I remember the door.

HENRY
Then my name got smaller.

She takes the chair that does not face the window. Henry notices this and sits behind neither chair nor desk. He rests against the filing cabinet.

HENRY
Coffee?

RUTH
At this hour?

HENRY
It's the same coffee as this morning.

RUTH
That wasn't my concern.

HENRY
Good.

He pours one cup. Ruth accepts it despite herself, smells it, and sets it on the floor.

HENRY
You came all this way to insult the office.

RUTH
No. I need you for dinner.

HENRY
That's not how people usually phrase it.

RUTH
I'm out of practice.

HENRY
With dinner?

RUTH
With needing you.

Henry waits. Ruth had prepared for a question. His silence makes her discard the answer.

RUTH
Tolland's. The Winter Room. Eight o'clock.

HENRY
The department store?

RUTH
After closing.

HENRY
Is it a robbery?

RUTH
It's a goodbye.

HENRY
Whose?

RUTH
Lydia Shaw is leaving Los Angeles tomorrow morning.

HENRY
For where?

RUTH
She says Portland.

HENRY
You don't believe her.

RUTH
I believe she's leaving. We disagree about what that means.

HENRY
What does she do?

RUTH
She alters things.

HENRY
Clothes?

RUTH
Mostly people. Clothes when they're watching.

Henry drinks the office coffee. It has declined during the conversation.

HENRY
Who invited me?

RUTH
I did.

HENRY
Who invited you to invite me?

RUTH
Edwin said I could bring one person.

HENRY
Who is Edwin?

RUTH
He runs the Winter Room. He believes seven is an accusation and eight is a dinner party.

HENRY
Did you tell him what I do?

RUTH
I told him you listen.

HENRY
That's not what I do.

RUTH
It's why you get paid.

HENRY
This is a job.

RUTH
No.

HENRY
You came to my office.

RUTH
You never gave me your home address.

HENRY
You never asked.

RUTH
I am asking you to dinner, Henry. Don't make both requests humiliating.

It is the first time she has used his given name. He lets the fact pass without pretending not to notice it.

HENRY
Why me?

RUTH
I need one person there who will not perform for them.

HENRY
People perform for me.

RUTH
Not twice.

HENRY
Who is “them”?

RUTH
People who love Lydia in incompatible ways.

HENRY
And you?

RUTH
I'm compatible with everyone until they ask.

HENRY
What do you think will happen?

RUTH
A woman will leave Los Angeles.

HENRY
That happens every day.

RUTH
Not when six people have built their lives around her staying.

Henry sets his coffee down beside hers. The two cups look like evidence from a meeting neither intends to admit occurred.

HENRY
What are you afraid of?

RUTH
She intends to forgive me.

HENRY
For what?

RUTH
She hasn't told me.

HENRY
Have you apologized?

RUTH
That's what makes it unpleasant.

HENRY
Decline.

RUTH
If I don't go, she'll forgive me in writing.

A traffic signal changes outside. Red light passes across the frosted glass, then is gone.

HENRY
Last time we were in a room together, you told the truth and hated me for hearing it.

RUTH
I hated everyone else for making it necessary.

HENRY
I was included.

RUTH
You usually are. You just stand far enough away to make it look accidental.

HENRY
You said you would rather I heard it from you than found it.

RUTH
I remember what I said.

HENRY
Does Frances still send you a card?

RUTH
At Christmas. To the old address.

Henry takes his coat from the hook.

HENRY
Is there food at this dinner?

RUTH
Edwin would consider the question actionable.

HENRY
I'll change my shirt.

RUTH
That is the closest thing to friendship you've ever said to me.

HENRY
Don't make me reconsider it.

Ruth picks up both coffee cups and carries them to the sink.

= Scene turn: Henry has accepted as Ruth's unfinished friend, but her reason for needing a witness remains deliberately incomplete.

## Scene 2 — Tolland's department store, after closing

= Present: Henry Calder, Ruth Ellery, Edwin Price, then Lydia Shaw.

= Immediate pressure: Henry enters a gathering in which his presence was permitted but not expected.

INT./EXT. TOLLAND'S DEPARTMENT STORE - EVENING

Tolland's motor court was designed so a customer could enter the store without touching the city. After closing, the dark porte cochere does the opposite. Each footstep returns from the stone.

The display windows are still illuminated. Inside them, mannequins attend dinners, descend painted staircases, and wait beside luggage that has never travelled. Beyond the glass, the real sales floor is dark.

Nearest the service door, one window is unfinished. Six mannequins face an empty seventh place beneath a white paper moon. Above the moon, where the scenery meets the ceiling of the window, a short black rectangle has been left open. A seamstress's chalk and a pair of scissors lie on the floor inside the glass.

HENRY
Is that Lydia's work?

RUTH
No.

Ruth presses a bell beside an unmarked service door.

HENRY
You know where the servants enter.

RUTH
Everybody who works for a living knows where the servants enter.

The door opens before Henry can answer. Edwin Price is narrow, silver-haired, and dressed with the severity of a man whose dining room has survived several owners.

EDWIN
You're early.

RUTH
By thirteen minutes.

EDWIN
For a departure, that is considered aggression.

RUTH
Edwin Price, Henry Calder.

EDWIN
Mr. Calder. I had understood you were a friend.

HENRY
So had I.

RUTH
We're trying it out.

Edwin considers Henry's coat, shoes, hands, and expression. It is not an inspection. It is seating preparation.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw has moved your place card twice.

HENRY
I didn't know I had one.

EDWIN
Neither did she. This has not prevented her.

He locks the door behind them and leads them up a short flight of stairs to the unlit cosmetics floor. In the dark, mirrors return fragments rather than people. Ruth knows the route and does not look at the counters. Henry does.

The passenger elevators are dark. Edwin unlocks a service lift.

EDWIN
Mr. Bell will send the others up this way from the Hill Street door. It is the only car running.

EDWIN
The dining room staff left at six. I have retained one cook and one waiter, both of whom have been promised ignorance.

HENRY
Can they deliver?

EDWIN
They are professionals.

The service lift rises through the store. Each floor appears briefly through the wire-glass panel: furniture arranged into rooms without walls, women's dresses hanging in patient rows, toys under canvas, lamps glowing alone because no one found the correct switch.

Ruth watches the floor numbers. Edwin watches Ruth.

EDWIN
She hasn't told me why she asked you.

RUTH
She hasn't told me either.

EDWIN
You misunderstand. Lydia has told me three reasons. She hasn't told me which one she plans to remember.

The lift stops.

The Winter Room curves along the top floor beneath a shallow glass roof. At lunchtime it seats a hundred and forty. Tonight a single round table occupies its center, laid for eight beneath low amber lamps. Los Angeles continues beyond the windows as a field of lights without an audience.

Lydia Shaw sits alone, writing place cards in green ink. She is dressed simply, but nothing about the dress appears accidental. One unopened suitcase stands beside the service bar.

She sees Ruth, then Henry. She finishes the name she is writing before she rises.

LYDIA
You brought a stranger.

RUTH
You told me not to come alone.

LYDIA
Those are not the same kindness.

RUTH
Lydia Shaw, Henry Calder.

Lydia offers Henry her hand. Her fingers carry two shallow pin marks and a stain of green ink.

LYDIA
What did she tell you about me?

HENRY
That you're leaving.

LYDIA
Then she told you the only thing no one here believes.

HENRY
Do you?

Lydia smiles—not because the question is clever, but because Henry has returned it without decorating it.

LYDIA
Sit beside me, Mr. Calder.

RUTH
He came with me.

LYDIA
That is why I would like to make it inconvenient.

An elevator bell sounds somewhere below. The empty store carries it upward, one clean note at a time.

Lydia looks toward the entrance. For the first time, her composure does not fail; it becomes work.

LYDIA
That will be the first person who thinks this dinner is about them.

= Scene turn: Henry has entered Lydia's arrangement and immediately become useful to both Lydia and Ruth for incompatible reasons. The next guest is arriving.
